  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Another frosty morning, especially inland but\nmilder mornings are just around the corner. Low-level \nthicknesses increasing supports steady warming mid to late week,\nand even though high pressure will remain in place, temps will \nwarm to above climo with 70s inland THU and FRI. For the OBX, \nhighs will be tempered by light northeast breezes off the cooler\nwaters of the Labrador Current as water temps have cooled to \nthe upper 50s. For tonight lows are expected to be AoB freezing\nin our coldest spots, but over the next few nights we won't be \nnearly as cold with lows in the 40s and 50s. \n\nIn addition to moderating temperatures, we will remain dry with\nhigh pressure settling overhead by the end of the week, and off\nthe coast by Saturday. Fire weather concerns may grow with the \ncuring vegetation combined with low RH values each afternoon. \nLuckily winds will not be an issue with a light gradient in \nplace. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The potential for a low to develop and gain \nstrength off the FL coast late this week in response to a \nshortwave digging across the Gulf above SFC coastal troughing \nongoing along the FL leg of the Gulfstream continues to grow. \nHowever, what happens once a pressure center presents itself \nremains fairly uncertain with the typical questions for a day 4+\nlow (that hasn't even formed yet) off the coast: where the \nsystem will track, how much it deepens, how fast is it moving, \netc... What structure/characteristics the low will have is also\nin question with NHC offering up 40% chance that this low could\ntake on subtropical, and maybe even tropical characteristics. \nThe key point here is: the impacts will not change based on what\nthis low ends up being called. Initially a generally Nward \nmotion is expected. Overnight and morning model suites still\nshow a wide swath of solutions, with some back and forth between\npossible solutions even within the same model. 12Z GFS remains \non the Wern envelope of possible solutions after the 06Z GFS\nlooked very similar to the euro, but this Wern/inland solution \nhas shifted a little further E compared to this time yesterday. \nOffshore solutions remain relatively similar to their previous \niterations. Details will become clearer over the next 48hrs as \nwe get into the HiRes window, get more data on the upper level\npattern progression, see how convection over the Gulf and Sern\nGulfstream plays out, and get closer to actual cyclogenesis. \nBottom line even though we are a day closer, nothing has been \nironed out! \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...King Tide Cycle starts second half of this \nweekend, peaking middle of next week. This higher than normal \nastronomical tide cycle is slightly stronger than the one that \noccurred the last week of OCT. Will have to see how the system \nin Key Message 2 progresses before we can determine what sort \nof effect this King Tide cycle will have on our coast.",
